proofs.proof_tex:
Write
\[
I_{a,s}=\mathbf 1\{G=a,S=s\},\qquad
p_{a,s}(x)=P(G=a,S=s\mid X=x),\qquad
\pi=P(G=g,S=1)=E_P[p_{g,1}(X)],
\]
and let \(\mu_{a,s}(x)=E_P[D\mid G=a,S=s,X=x]\).  In the one-period target under consideration, the definitions of \(q\) and \(\theta\) become
\[
q(x)=\mu_{g,1}(x)-\mu_{g,0}(x)-\mu_{c,1}(x)+\mu_{c,0}(x),
\qquad
\theta=\frac{E_P[p_{g,1}(X)q(X)]}{\pi}.
\tag{R1}
\]
Retained-cell overlap gives \(p_{a,s}(X)\ge\varepsilon\) almost surely for all four cells and \(\pi\ge\varepsilon>0\), so every division below is legitimate.  Definition \(\ref{def:pairwise-score}\), with \(D=\Delta Y_g\), specializes to
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Psi_{k,c,P}
=\frac1\pi\bigg[&I_{g,1}\{q(X)-\theta+D-\mu_{g,1}(X)\}
-I_{g,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{g,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{g,0}(X)\}\\
&-I_{c,1}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,1}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,1}(X)\}
+I_{c,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,0}(X)\}\bigg].
\end{aligned}
\tag{R2}
\]

We first reduce the Ortiz--Villavicencio--Sant'Anna formula.  After relabeling their target cohort \(2\) as \(g\), their never-treated cohort \(\infty\) as \(c\), and their eligibility indicator as \(S\), Lemma \(\ref{lem:causal-identification}\) identifies their \(\operatorname{ATT}(2,2)\) with \(\theta\) in (R1).  For every cell \((a,s)\), conditional expectation and overlap give
\[
\begin{aligned}
E_P\!\left[I_{a,s}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{a,s}(X)}\right]
&=E_P\!\left[E_P[I_{a,s}\mid X]
       \frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{a,s}(X)}\right]\\
&=E_P[p_{g,1}(X)]=\pi.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R3}
\]
Thus the target weight in the cited OVS formula is \(I_{g,1}/\pi\), and each comparison weight is
\(I_{a,s}p_{g,1}(X)/\{\pi p_{a,s}(X)\}\).  The target pseudo-outcome in that formula obeys
\[
\begin{aligned}
&D-\mu_{g,0}(X)-\mu_{c,1}(X)+\mu_{c,0}(X)-\theta\\
&\qquad=D-\mu_{g,1}(X)+q(X)-\theta,
\end{aligned}
\tag{R4}
\]
where the equality is (R1).  Substituting (R3)--(R4) into the efficient influence function transcribed in Lemma \(\ref{lem:ovs-two-period-panel-ddd-eif}\) gives respectively the target, target-ineligible, comparison-eligible, and comparison-ineligible terms of (R2), with signs \(+,-,-,+\).  Hence that published influence function equals \(\Psi_{k,c,P}\) term by term.

For the Akbari--Kiyavash--Ghassami formula, relabel \(G_A=1\) as \(G=g\), \(G_A=0\) as \(G=c\), and \(D_A\) as \(S\).  Their target is, by conditional expectation and (R1),
\[
\psi_{\mathrm{pd}}
=E_P[q(X)\mid G=g,S=1]
=\frac{E_P[p_{g,1}(X)q(X)]}{\pi}
=\theta.
\tag{R5}
\]
Under the same relabeling, \(G_AD_A=I_{g,1}\) and
\(\pi_{r,a,s}(X)=p_{g,1}(X)/p_{a,s}(X)\).  Therefore the efficient influence function transcribed in Lemma \(\ref{lem:akg-panel-ddd-eif}\) becomes
\[
\eta_A
=\frac1\pi\sum_{a,s\in\{0,1\}}(-1)^{a+s+1}
\left\{I_{g,1}-I_{a,s}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{a,s}(X)}\right\}
\{D-\mu_{a,s}(X)\}
-\frac{I_{g,1}}\pi\theta,
\tag{R6}
\]
where \(a=1\) denotes \(g\), \(a=0\) denotes \(c\), and the second coordinate is eligibility.  Put \(\sigma_{a,s}=(-1)^{a+s+1}\).  In the order \((0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)\), these signs are \((-1,+1,+1,-1)\), and hence \(\sum_{a,s}\sigma_{a,s}=0\).  The part of (R6) multiplying the common indicator \(I_{g,1}\) is consequently
\[
I_{g,1}\sum_{a,s}\sigma_{a,s}\{D-\mu_{a,s}(X)\}
=-I_{g,1}\sum_{a,s}\sigma_{a,s}\mu_{a,s}(X)
=I_{g,1}q(X),
\tag{R7}
\]
by (R1).  The cell-specific part has coefficients \(-\sigma_{a,s}=(+1,-1,-1,+1)\).  In particular, its \((a,s)=(1,1)\) term is \(I_{g,1}\{D-\mu_{g,1}(X)\}\), because its propensity ratio is one.  Substitution into (R6) therefore gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\eta_A=\frac1\pi\bigg[&I_{g,1}\{q(X)-\theta+D-\mu_{g,1}(X)\}
-I_{g,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{g,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{g,0}(X)\}\\
&-I_{c,1}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,1}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,1}(X)\}
+I_{c,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,0}(X)\}\bigg]
=\Psi_{k,c,P}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R8}
\]
This establishes the second term-by-term equality, including the denominator derivative \(-I_{g,1}\theta/\pi\).

In the stated two-cohort design, the published clean comparison set at the sole post-treatment period contains only \(c\).  There are therefore no two distinct clean cohorts and hence no nonzero row in \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{OVS}}\).  By the zero-dimensional convention in Definition \(\ref{def:ovs-normal-projection}\),
\[
Z_P^{\mathrm{OVS}}=0,
\qquad p_{\mathrm{OVS},P}=0.
\tag{R9}
\]
For one target with \(\omega_k=1\), the definition of the reference gradient gives \(\psi_P^0=\Psi_{k,c,P}\).  Equations (R9) and the tangent-exhaustion conclusion of Theorem \(\ref{thm:complete-normal-canonical-gradient}\) then give
\[
\phi_{\mathrm{OVS},P}=\psi_P^0=\Psi_{k,c,P},
\]
and establish that this common gradient is canonical in the present published observed-law model.  For an arbitrary scalar \(\omega_k\), the target pathwise derivative and each displayed influence function are multiplied by \(\omega_k\), proving the scaling assertion.

Finally, Lemma \(\ref{lem:csx-staggered-did-eif-scope}\) records that Chen--Sant'Anna--Xie's observed vector has no eligibility coordinate and that their maintained restrictions are DiD rather than the present four-cell eligibility-gap equalities.  The algebra above constructs no map between those tangent spaces.  It therefore proves the two panel-DDD reductions and no nesting relation with that distinct staggered-DiD model.
